From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:14:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/apple: m4: Refuse a sleep that powers the SoC down

The T8140 display processors now have a stop and a warm restart for
suspend-to-RAM. T8132's M4 display firmware has none yet: its suspend
sends setPowerState(0) and leaves the coprocessor running with its power
domains up, which is right for suspend-to-idle but not for a sleep whose
commit powers the SoC down underneath it. The resume would then talk to a
firmware that lost its power.

Refuse such a sleep (pm_suspend_via_firmware(), set only by apple-s2r's
->begin) with -EBUSY while the M4 DCP is bound, as asahi's G16 already
does. The system stays up and the PM core resumes what it suspended.
Suspend-to-idle takes the path it took before.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
index 459c6d6e91..57bbbe1d20 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
@@ -8324,6 +8324,16 @@ static int dcp_platform_suspend(struct device *dev)
 	if (pm_suspend_via_firmware() && dcp_s2r_ext_capable(dcp))
 		return dcp_s2r_ext_suspend(dcp);
 
+	/*
+	 * T8132 has no firmware stop for a sleep that powers the SoC down: the
+	 * coprocessor below keeps running, and that sleep would cut its power
+	 * under it. Refuse it, as asahi does for G16; suspend-to-idle goes on.
+	 */
+	if (pm_suspend_via_firmware() && iomfb_is_m4(dcp)) {
+		dev_err(dev, "no display firmware stop on this SoC, refusing the sleep\n");
+		return -EBUSY;
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * T8132: setPowerState powers the display down; the coprocessor keeps
 	 * running and its power domains stay on, as below, unless the internal
